WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.100
A newborn mouse that lacks the muscle kinase MuSK cannot breathe.

2
00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.260
Its motor axons reach the diaphragm.

3
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.300
So why does no junction form?

4
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:16.793
A synapse is not built whenever an axon touches a target.

5
00:00:17.833 --> 00:00:22.673
At a mature junction, the acetylcholine receptors are packed under the nerve ending.

6
00:00:23.713 --> 00:00:25.453
Along the rest of the fibre, they are sparse.

7
00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:29.193
So building a junction is a sorting problem.

8
00:00:30.113 --> 00:00:32.193
Where do the receptors go, and who tells them?

9
00:00:35.267 --> 00:00:38.707
The first clue came from frog muscle, in 1978.

10
00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:42.387
Fibres and nerve terminals were made to degenerate.

11
00:00:43.267 --> 00:00:45.727
Empty sheaths of basal lamina were left.

12
00:00:46.647 --> 00:00:49.707
Regenerating axons returned to the old synaptic sites.

13
00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:53.817
The basal lamina remembered where the synapse had been.

14
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.510
McMahan's group searched the electric organ of the ray Torpedo.

15
00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:02.130
In 1987 they named the protein agrin.

16
00:01:03.133 --> 00:01:07.193
Motor neurons send their own form of agrin into the basal lamina.

17
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:13.093
Mice lacking MuSK, a muscle receptor tyrosine kinase, make no junctions.

18
00:01:14.173 --> 00:01:16.573
Agrin acts through a complex that contains MuSK.

19
00:01:17.417 --> 00:01:19.657
Yet MuSK alone does not bind agrin.

20
00:01:20.497 --> 00:01:22.137
What kind of molecule would you look for?

21
00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:32.527
A protein on the muscle surface that binds agrin and joins MuSK.

22
00:01:33.147 --> 00:01:34.867
That protein is Lrp4.

23
00:01:35.817 --> 00:01:40.617
Cells that are not muscle bind neural agrin once they are given Lrp4.

24
00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:45.847
Inside the muscle, Dok7 binds activated MuSK and boosts it.

25
00:01:46.607 --> 00:01:49.987
Rapsyn, a scaffold under the membrane, holds the receptors together.

26
00:01:51.067 --> 00:01:56.927
The chain runs: neural agrin, Lrp4, MuSK with Dok7, rapsyn, then clustered

27
00:01:56.927 --> 00:01:57.927
receptors.

28
00:02:00.917 --> 00:02:04.037
Remove MuSK or Dok7, and no junctions form.

29
00:02:04.937 --> 00:02:07.797
Without rapsyn, receptors are made but never clustered.

30
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:11.960
Remove agrin, and fewer, smaller clusters remain.

31
00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:14.140
Hold on to that odd result.

32
00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.030
Here is the idea for this lesson.

33
00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:20.030
The muscle proposes; the nerve disposes.

34
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.850
The muscle builds clusters with its own machinery.

35
00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:26.070
The nerve decides which stay.

36
00:02:27.083 --> 00:02:31.863
Agrin, held under the terminal, raises MuSK activity there and protects those

37
00:02:31.863 --> 00:02:35.103
clusters. Acetylcholine disperses the rest.

38
00:02:36.283 --> 00:02:41.063
Takeaway: without MuSK, the muscle cannot build clusters, so no junction forms.

39
00:02:41.963 --> 00:02:44.703
When it can build, the nerve decides which clusters stay.
